1
00:00:03,710 --> 00:00:05,780
Hey, will you steam my uniform next?

2
00:00:05,780 --> 00:00:07,300
Yeah, w... interesting.

3
00:00:07,300 --> 00:00:09,010
Do you recall this conversation?

4
00:00:09,020 --> 00:00:11,380
"Leonard, want to go halfsies on a steamer?"

5
00:00:11,380 --> 00:00:14,090
"No, Sheldon, we don't need a steamer."

6
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:17,900
Looks like that rumpled chicken's come home to roost.

7
00:00:20,210 --> 00:00:21,580
Here are the make-up sponges you asked for.

8
00:00:21,580 --> 00:00:23,530
Oh, thanks, I thought I had more.

9
00:00:27,900 --> 00:00:30,300
Damn, you've got more makeup than I do.

10
00:00:30,910 --> 00:00:33,420
You've got better makeup than I do.

11
00:00:33,420 --> 00:00:34,710
Yeah, I'm borrowing this.

12
00:00:35,900 --> 00:00:37,990
This is my Comic-Con makeup.

13
00:00:37,990 --> 00:00:39,380
I love you, but there are some things

14
00:00:39,380 --> 00:00:41,940
a man doesn't share with his girlfriend.

15
00:00:42,620 --> 00:00:44,290
That's a wise policy.

16
00:00:44,320 --> 00:00:47,350
I once borrowed my sister's makeup for a costume contest.

17
00:00:47,350 --> 00:00:49,200
Got a terrible case of pinkeye.

18
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:51,060
Yeah, but luckily, I was going as a zombie.

19
00:00:51,060 --> 00:00:52,640
I won second place.

20
00:00:53,990 --> 00:00:55,790
I feel like you guys just went to Comic-Con.

21
00:00:55,790 --> 00:00:57,050
That was San Diego Comic-Con.

22
00:00:57,050 --> 00:00:59,200
This is Bakersfield Comic-Con.

23
00:00:59,740 --> 00:01:01,110
Is that better?

24
00:01:01,110 --> 00:01:02,050
Well, it's a lot smaller.

25
00:01:02,050 --> 00:01:03,330
It's more about the comic books.

26
00:01:03,330 --> 00:01:04,640
The way these conventions used to be

27
00:01:04,640 --> 00:01:06,330
before they went all Hollywood.

28
00:01:06,330 --> 00:01:09,450
So to answer your question, no, it's not better.

29
00:01:10,270 --> 00:01:12,120
Well, then why are you going?

30
00:01:12,120 --> 00:01:13,980
It's a comic book convention.

31
00:01:13,980 --> 00:01:16,190
You know, it's like pizza or particle accelerators,

32
00:01:16,190 --> 00:01:18,600
even the stinky one's still pretty good.

33
00:01:19,230 --> 00:01:19,740
All right.

34
00:01:19,740 --> 00:01:20,830
Well, you guys have fun.

35
00:01:20,830 --> 00:01:22,100
I guess I'll see you Sunday night.

36
00:01:22,100 --> 00:01:23,060
- Yeah. - Okay.

37
00:01:23,060 --> 00:01:24,440
Oh, hang on a second.

38
00:01:24,440 --> 00:01:26,600
Hold this.

39
00:01:31,240 --> 00:01:32,120
What was that for?

40
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:34,370
To show people when they don't believe me.

41
00:02:02,450 --> 00:02:03,580
Oh, this is the best.

42
00:02:03,580 --> 00:02:05,380
You have booze with breakfast on a Tuesday,

43
00:02:05,380 --> 00:02:06,480
you got a problem.

44
00:02:06,480 --> 00:02:08,340
You do it on the weekend, you got brunch.

45
00:02:09,310 --> 00:02:11,140
Sheldon doesn't believe in brunch.

46
00:02:11,140 --> 00:02:12,370
He can't stand being at a table

47
00:02:12,370 --> 00:02:13,690
where one person's having an omelet

48
00:02:13,690 --> 00:02:15,930
and another person's having a sandwich.

49
00:02:16,620 --> 00:02:18,980
He's not exhausting at all.

50
00:02:23,350 --> 00:02:24,990
It's Leonard, he says they're on the road

51
00:02:24,990 --> 00:02:27,890
and headed for Bakersfield at "warp speed."

52
00:02:28,450 --> 00:02:29,290
Maybe it's the Mimosa,

53
00:02:29,290 --> 00:02:31,570
but I'm gonna give the kid an LOL.

54
00:02:33,340 --> 00:02:34,680
It's cute how excited they are.

55
00:02:34,680 --> 00:02:35,950
You should have seen Howard

56
00:02:35,950 --> 00:02:37,710
sewing his costume all week for the convention.

57
00:02:37,720 --> 00:02:39,720
When did Howard learn to sew?

58
00:02:39,720 --> 00:02:41,350
When he was a little boy, every couple months,

59
00:02:41,350 --> 00:02:43,260
he would have to let his mom's pants out.

60
00:02:47,150 --> 00:02:49,630
I don't even understand why they go to these conventions.

61
00:02:49,630 --> 00:02:50,310
I know.

62
00:02:50,310 --> 00:02:51,850
The four of them work at a major university.

63
00:02:51,850 --> 00:02:53,010
They're all super smart.

64
00:02:53,010 --> 00:02:55,530
How can they still be into something made for 12-year-olds?

65
00:02:55,530 --> 00:02:56,970
I don't mind it.

66
00:02:56,970 --> 00:02:59,270
I think Howie's just in touch with his inner child.

67
00:02:59,270 --> 00:03:01,370
Although when he comes to bed in his Batman pajamas,

68
00:03:01,370 --> 00:03:04,720
sometimes it feels like I'm touching his inner child.

69
00:03:07,040 --> 00:03:09,310
It's probably because they were bullied growing up.

70
00:03:09,310 --> 00:03:11,080
In a world where you can't fight back,

71
00:03:11,080 --> 00:03:13,820
superheroes provide meaningful wish-fulfillment.

72
00:03:14,500 --> 00:03:17,380
Now I feel bad for picking on all those kids.

73
00:03:17,380 --> 00:03:18,660
Although, in my defense,

74
00:03:18,660 --> 00:03:20,630
if Danny Biffle didn't want to eat a mouthful of dirt,

75
00:03:20,630 --> 00:03:23,660
he shouldn't have shown up to school wearing a bow tie.

76
00:03:24,810 --> 00:03:27,010
I've never even read a comic book. You guys?

77
00:03:27,010 --> 00:03:28,240
- Uh-uh. - No.

78
00:03:28,240 --> 00:03:30,150
I mean, it's such an important part of their lives.

79
00:03:30,150 --> 00:03:31,730
Maybe we should try reading some.

80
00:03:31,740 --> 00:03:33,130
Seriously?

81
00:03:34,200 --> 00:03:36,300
The comic book store isn't far from here.

82
00:03:36,300 --> 00:03:39,120
That is an amazing idea.

83
00:03:39,120 --> 00:03:41,420
Okay, how many of these have I had?

84
00:03:47,280 --> 00:03:49,520
I think you should turn on the GPS.

85
00:03:49,520 --> 00:03:50,750
It is on.

86
00:03:50,750 --> 00:03:53,910
But the turn-by-turn voice option isn't on.

87
00:03:53,910 --> 00:03:55,380
I know I'd feel more safe

88
00:03:55,380 --> 00:03:57,250
if you turn on the turn-by-turn voice option.

89
00:03:57,250 --> 00:04:00,140
I love the turn-by-turn voice option.

90
00:04:00,140 --> 00:04:03,180
Has it really only been ten miles?

91
00:04:04,320 --> 00:04:06,940
I'm turning it on, but just to shut you up.

92
00:04:08,100 --> 00:04:09,940
Leonard, bear left

93
00:04:09,940 --> 00:04:13,600
and continue on Interstate 210.

94
00:04:15,150 --> 00:04:18,150
Ooh, sounds like that fellow knows what he's talking about.

95
00:04:18,150 --> 00:04:21,310
I'd put on my listening ears if I were you.

96
00:04:21,310 --> 00:04:23,130
What did you do?

97
00:04:23,130 --> 00:04:25,010
I found a hack online.

98
00:04:25,010 --> 00:04:26,860
I was able to upload MP3 recordings

99
00:04:26,860 --> 00:04:29,090
of my voice to your GPS.

100
00:04:29,090 --> 00:04:30,290
That is so cool.

101
00:04:30,290 --> 00:04:32,260
Counterpoint, no, it's not.

102
00:04:33,760 --> 00:04:36,130
Continue on Interstate 210

103
00:04:36,130 --> 00:04:37,890
for five miles.

104
00:04:37,890 --> 00:04:41,530
Here's an interesting fact about interstates.

105
00:04:42,380 --> 00:04:43,220
Really?

106
00:04:43,220 --> 00:04:46,090
Shh, he said it's interesting.

107
00:04:46,800 --> 00:04:49,540
Interstates are numbered as follows.

108
00:04:49,540 --> 00:04:53,070
Even-numbered routes run east and west,

109
00:04:53,760 --> 00:04:57,330
odd-numbered routes run north and south.

110
00:04:57,980 --> 00:05:01,340
Three-digit route numbers indicate bypasses

111
00:05:01,340 --> 00:05:03,530
or spurs.

112
00:05:05,170 --> 00:05:06,660
Look, Leonard, there's a bridge.

113
00:05:06,660 --> 00:05:08,430
Drive off it.

114
00:05:09,420 --> 00:05:12,190
You know, we're not that far from Vazquez Rocks.

115
00:05:12,190 --> 00:05:15,480
Oh, they shot a lot of Star Trek episodes out there.

116
00:05:15,480 --> 00:05:17,580
We've got our costumes in the trunk.

117
00:05:17,580 --> 00:05:20,090
We could go there and have a little photo shoot.

118
00:05:20,090 --> 00:05:21,480
Great idea.

119
00:05:21,480 --> 00:05:23,780
I haven't had a carbohydrate in two weeks.

120
00:05:23,780 --> 00:05:26,020
These cheekbones need to be in front of a camera

121
00:05:26,020 --> 00:05:28,400
before I eat a pretzel and they're gone.

122
00:05:29,090 --> 00:05:30,200
Yeah, that sounds fun.

123
00:05:30,200 --> 00:05:31,950
Oh, smashing.

124
00:05:31,950 --> 00:05:34,790
- Now, Leonard, do you know how to get there? - No.

125
00:05:34,790 --> 00:05:37,680
Yeah, well luckily, someone in the car does.

126
00:05:37,680 --> 00:05:39,690
Recalculating.

127
00:05:40,760 --> 00:05:42,240
While we're waiting,

128
00:05:42,240 --> 00:05:44,460
do you know which president signed

129
00:05:44,460 --> 00:05:48,370
the Intestate Highway System Act into law?

130
00:05:48,370 --> 00:05:52,040
The answer, coming up in 14 miles.

131
00:05:53,410 --> 00:05:54,980
None of you will get it.

132
00:05:54,980 --> 00:05:56,690
It's Eisenhower.

133
00:06:10,390 --> 00:06:12,060
Why are they staring?

134
00:06:13,290 --> 00:06:13,850
Who cares?

135
00:06:13,850 --> 00:06:15,790
Just soak it in.

136
00:06:17,550 --> 00:06:18,920
Hello, boys.

137
00:06:25,640 --> 00:06:27,890
Could you please stop staring? They're just girls.

138
00:06:27,890 --> 00:06:31,110
It's nothing you haven't seen in movies or in drawings.

139
00:06:32,540 --> 00:06:33,720
Hey, Stuart.

140
00:06:33,720 --> 00:06:35,160
Wh-What brings you guys here?

141
00:06:35,160 --> 00:06:37,640
We were looking for a recommendation about comic books.

142
00:06:37,820 --> 00:06:41,050
Oh, well, I recommend you don't open a store and sell them.

143
00:06:41,820 --> 00:06:42,670
No, we were just wondering

144
00:06:42,670 --> 00:06:44,040
why the guys like this stuff so much,

145
00:06:44,040 --> 00:06:45,410
so we thought we'd give it a try.

146
00:06:45,410 --> 00:06:47,720
Oh, okay. What do you think you might be into?

147
00:06:47,720 --> 00:06:50,470
Superhero, fantasy, graphic novels, manga--

148
00:06:50,470 --> 00:06:53,270
I swear I will turn a hose on you.

149
00:06:58,310 --> 00:07:00,160
What kind of comics do the guys like?

150
00:07:00,160 --> 00:07:02,150
Um, a little bit of everything.

151
00:07:02,150 --> 00:07:03,700
Mostly superhero stuff.

152
00:07:03,700 --> 00:07:05,880
All right, well, who's the best superhero?

153
00:07:07,160 --> 00:07:08,750
You can't ask a question like that in here.

154
00:07:08,750 --> 00:07:10,770
Are you trying to start a rumble?

155
00:07:12,320 --> 00:07:14,410
Well, what do you recommend?

156
00:07:14,410 --> 00:07:15,740
Well, uh, let's see.

157
00:07:15,740 --> 00:07:17,750
You've got your basic clean-cut good guys--

158
00:07:17,750 --> 00:07:20,520
Superman, Spider-Man, Captain America.

159
00:07:20,520 --> 00:07:22,590
Then you have your darker anti-heroes--

160
00:07:22,590 --> 00:07:24,750
your Batman, your Wolverine, Punisher.

161
00:07:24,750 --> 00:07:26,930
Ooh, I do love a bad boy.

162
00:07:28,960 --> 00:07:32,430
As evidenced by your boyfriend and his fear of hamsters.

163
00:07:34,840 --> 00:07:36,970
If I were you, I'd go for Fables number one.

164
00:07:36,970 --> 00:07:38,400
The artwork is sophisticated,

165
00:07:38,400 --> 00:07:39,610
it's intelligently written,

166
00:07:39,610 --> 00:07:41,840
and it doesn't objectify or stereotype women.

167
00:07:41,840 --> 00:07:42,840
Ooh, Thor!

168
00:07:42,840 --> 00:07:44,370
He's hot.

169
00:07:45,670 --> 00:07:47,680
Yeah, he kind of is.

170
00:07:53,280 --> 00:07:55,000
And we're blending,

171
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:56,850
and we're blending,

172
00:07:56,850 --> 00:07:59,220
and we're done.

173
00:08:03,230 --> 00:08:05,700
I know Mr. Data isn't supposed to smile,

174
00:08:05,700 --> 00:08:07,530
but here it comes.

175
00:08:11,730 --> 00:08:13,300
Come on, guys. Let's do this.

176
00:08:13,300 --> 00:08:15,840
Yeah, I'm sweating my bald cap off.

177
00:08:21,410 --> 00:08:22,880
So what's our first pose going to be?

178
00:08:22,880 --> 00:08:25,670
I say we begin with a classic Star Trek fight scene.

179
00:08:25,670 --> 00:08:27,420
I'll set the timer.

180
00:08:33,180 --> 00:08:35,480
Sheldon, how is that a fight pose?

181
00:08:35,480 --> 00:08:38,070
Mr. Data's weapon is his mind.

182
00:08:38,070 --> 00:08:39,910
I'm wielding it.

183
00:08:47,510 --> 00:08:49,560
Phasers on the Borg!

184
00:08:52,200 --> 00:08:54,120
Charlie's Angels.

185
00:08:57,650 --> 00:08:59,110
Okay, what's next?

186
00:08:59,110 --> 00:09:01,670
Now let's do some sexy glamour shots.

187
00:09:02,210 --> 00:09:03,470
I'll set the mood.

188
00:09:12,250 --> 00:09:14,190
Yeah, nice one.

189
00:09:15,870 --> 00:09:17,240
That's right.

190
00:09:20,220 --> 00:09:20,780
Oh, my God.

191
00:09:20,780 --> 00:09:22,700
Leonard, someone's stealing your car!

192
00:09:22,700 --> 00:09:25,140
What? Hey, hey!

193
00:09:25,140 --> 00:09:26,470
Come back here! Stop!

194
00:09:26,470 --> 00:09:29,030
Stealing is against the law!

195
00:09:31,370 --> 00:09:32,790
I don't believe this.

196
00:09:32,790 --> 00:09:34,360
Son of a bitch.

197
00:09:34,360 --> 00:09:36,180
I'll call 911.

198
00:09:36,180 --> 00:09:37,130
Wha...

199
00:09:37,130 --> 00:09:39,580
Oh, no, my phone is in my other pants.

200
00:09:39,580 --> 00:09:41,350
- Oh, so is mine. - Mine, too.

201
00:09:41,350 --> 00:09:42,970
Anybody got any ideas?

202
00:09:42,970 --> 00:09:44,180
Nope.

203
00:09:44,180 --> 00:09:46,950
The only thing left to do now is assign blame.

204
00:09:46,950 --> 00:09:48,490
Nice going.

205
00:09:54,710 --> 00:09:58,580
What kind of a person steals another person's car in broad daylight?

206
00:09:58,580 --> 00:10:01,180
What kind of person leaves his keys in the car?

207
00:10:01,180 --> 00:10:03,380
I thought we agreed this was all Koothrappali's fault.

208
00:10:03,380 --> 00:10:04,620
You're right.

209
00:10:04,620 --> 00:10:06,080
Nice going.

210
00:10:07,980 --> 00:10:09,010
Car.

211
00:10:16,130 --> 00:10:18,400
What is wrong with people? Why don't they stop?

212
00:10:18,400 --> 00:10:19,930
Maybe we're better off.

213
00:10:19,930 --> 00:10:22,630
You know, what if we were to get in a car with a crazy person?

214
00:10:22,630 --> 00:10:24,330
Look at us, Sheldon.

215
00:10:24,340 --> 00:10:25,890
We're the crazy people.

216
00:10:28,670 --> 00:10:31,240
Well, perhaps we should hold up a sign

217
00:10:31,240 --> 00:10:35,100
that assures passing motorists of our mental competence.

218
00:10:36,430 --> 00:10:39,330
Good idea. Why don't you get started on that?

219
00:10:40,930 --> 00:10:42,690
Come on, let's just start walking.

220
00:10:42,690 --> 00:10:45,800
There's got to be a gas station or something nearby.

221
00:10:45,800 --> 00:10:47,580
What, you think just because you're wearing a captain's uniform,

222
00:10:47,580 --> 00:10:49,930
you're in charge?

223
00:10:49,930 --> 00:10:51,570
Yes.

224
00:10:52,260 --> 00:10:53,190
All right.

225
00:11:04,150 --> 00:11:06,150
Hmm, okay, I'm done.

226
00:11:06,150 --> 00:11:08,490
How did you guys finish so fast?

227
00:11:08,490 --> 00:11:10,110
I don't know, there were a lot of pictures,

228
00:11:10,110 --> 00:11:13,780
and one page only had the word "Brakkadoom!"

229
00:11:16,040 --> 00:11:18,470
Yeah, well, I have street smarts.

230
00:11:19,480 --> 00:11:21,010
So, what'd you guys think?

231
00:11:21,010 --> 00:11:23,660
Well, there was a lot of action,

232
00:11:23,660 --> 00:11:26,460
and the story moved along at a brisk pace.

233
00:11:26,460 --> 00:11:28,210
It was, overall...

234
00:11:28,210 --> 00:11:30,080
what's the word I'm looking for?

235
00:11:30,080 --> 00:11:31,910
- Stupid? - So stupid.

236
00:11:32,970 --> 00:11:34,930
I don't know how Leonard can get so caught up in this.

237
00:11:35,210 --> 00:11:36,430
It's crazy; they spend hours

238
00:11:36,430 --> 00:11:38,610
arguing about things that don't even exist.

239
00:11:38,610 --> 00:11:40,310
- What a waste of time. - I know.

240
00:11:40,310 --> 00:11:43,030
A hammer so heavy that no one else can pick it up?

241
00:11:45,260 --> 00:11:46,600
I don't think it's heavy.

242
00:11:46,600 --> 00:11:47,800
I think it's some sort of magic,

243
00:11:47,800 --> 00:11:49,670
so only Thor can lift it.

244
00:11:49,670 --> 00:11:51,820
That makes even less sense, I mean...

245
00:11:51,820 --> 00:11:55,340
No, no, no, it doesn't.

246
00:11:56,130 --> 00:11:57,430
Thor is a god.

247
00:11:57,430 --> 00:11:59,750
The hammer is his; only he can use it.

248
00:11:59,750 --> 00:12:02,710
It's like Sheldon and his toothbrush.

249
00:12:03,800 --> 00:12:06,290
Or his thin, beckoning lips.

250
00:12:07,370 --> 00:12:08,430
Okay, hang on,

251
00:12:08,430 --> 00:12:10,420
what if Thor's hand is on the hammer?

252
00:12:10,420 --> 00:12:12,320
I mean, if he's touching it with his god magic,

253
00:12:12,320 --> 00:12:14,040
does that mean I could lift it?

254
00:12:14,040 --> 00:12:15,650
- No. - Yes.

255
00:12:17,170 --> 00:12:19,210
Well, which is it?

256
00:12:19,210 --> 00:12:21,070
Maybe we missed something.

257
00:12:21,070 --> 00:12:22,290
Let's read it again.

258
00:12:22,290 --> 00:12:24,020
- Okay. - Yeah.

259
00:12:30,130 --> 00:12:31,430
You want some tea?

260
00:12:31,430 --> 00:12:33,220
Good idea. I'll help you.

261
00:12:34,570 --> 00:12:36,050
Wait, I thought we were reading.

262
00:12:36,050 --> 00:12:37,200
We are.

263
00:12:37,200 --> 00:12:40,780
We're just, uh, giving you a head start.

264
00:12:50,830 --> 00:12:52,730
I wish my mom was here.

265
00:12:52,730 --> 00:12:55,300
We could all hang out in her shadow.

266
00:12:57,270 --> 00:12:59,350
Wool pants in the desert.

267
00:12:59,350 --> 00:13:02,300
I feel like I've got poached testicles.

268
00:13:04,330 --> 00:13:05,700
Oh, you poor thing;

269
00:13:05,700 --> 00:13:06,810
you're sweating.

270
00:13:06,810 --> 00:13:09,540
That's so much worse than having your car stolen.

271
00:13:09,540 --> 00:13:11,450
Insurance is gonna buy you a new car.

272
00:13:11,450 --> 00:13:14,670
It's not gonna de-funk my junk.

273
00:13:15,380 --> 00:13:16,730
Gentlemen,

274
00:13:16,730 --> 00:13:18,760
a little less bellyaching.

275
00:13:18,760 --> 00:13:20,290
We're Starfleet officers

276
00:13:20,290 --> 00:13:22,430
and a member of the Borg Collective.

277
00:13:22,430 --> 00:13:25,490
Please, Sheldon, I am so not in the mood.

278
00:13:25,490 --> 00:13:26,890
Leonard,

279
00:13:26,900 --> 00:13:29,200
all our lives we have dreamed

280
00:13:29,200 --> 00:13:33,380
of finding ourselves inside one of the fantasy worlds we love.

281
00:13:33,380 --> 00:13:35,070
And look at us.

282
00:13:35,070 --> 00:13:37,090
At this moment, we are, in fact,

283
00:13:37,090 --> 00:13:39,000
a Star Trek landing party

284
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:43,100
stranded in an alien and unforgiving environment,

285
00:13:43,100 --> 00:13:45,950
relying only on our wits, our fortitude

286
00:13:45,950 --> 00:13:47,610
and our moxie.

287
00:13:47,610 --> 00:13:49,680
As long as we have those things,

288
00:13:49,680 --> 00:13:51,340
- nothing can stop... - Nerds!

289
00:13:58,250 --> 00:14:00,030
I hate this planet.

290
00:14:05,720 --> 00:14:07,460
It says right here on the hammer,

291
00:14:07,460 --> 00:14:09,900
"Whoever holds this hammer, if he be worthy,

292
00:14:09,900 --> 00:14:11,680
shall possess the power of Thor."

293
00:14:11,680 --> 00:14:13,510
Hold on, who decides who's worthy?

294
00:14:13,510 --> 00:14:14,900
Does the hammer decide?

295
00:14:14,900 --> 00:14:16,030
- Yes! - No!

296
00:14:18,570 --> 00:14:19,710
It can't decide.

297
00:14:19,710 --> 00:14:20,410
It's a hammer.

298
00:14:20,410 --> 00:14:22,140
You said it's a magic hammer.

299
00:14:22,140 --> 00:14:23,960
Yeah, it can't make decisions.

300
00:14:23,960 --> 00:14:25,350
If Harry Potter's wand can make decisions,

301
00:14:25,350 --> 00:14:27,320
why can't Thor's hammer?

302
00:14:27,320 --> 00:14:30,610
Okay, if you're going to start comparing wands and hammers,

303
00:14:30,610 --> 00:14:32,800
I can't even take you seriously.

304
00:14:50,870 --> 00:14:52,290
Hello.

305
00:14:53,670 --> 00:14:54,890
Hello.

306
00:14:56,590 --> 00:15:00,320
Uh, four glasses of water, please.

307
00:15:01,040 --> 00:15:02,940
Anything for you guys?

308
00:15:03,920 --> 00:15:06,960
Can I use your phone? Our car got stolen.

309
00:15:06,960 --> 00:15:09,620
Why don't you ask Scotty to beam you up?

310
00:15:12,460 --> 00:15:14,740
Scotty was on the original series,

311
00:15:14,740 --> 00:15:17,530
and we're Next Generation.

312
00:15:17,530 --> 00:15:18,990
So...

313
00:15:18,990 --> 00:15:20,750
joke's on you.

314
00:15:24,150 --> 00:15:26,330
We're not with him.

315
00:15:30,680 --> 00:15:32,980
You can't pick something up in outer space.

316
00:15:32,980 --> 00:15:35,770
- In space, there is no up. - Oh, yeah?

317
00:15:35,770 --> 00:15:38,570
Then how does the sun come "Up" every day?

318
00:15:40,430 --> 00:15:42,860
Hard to argue with those kind of street smarts.

319
00:15:48,030 --> 00:15:50,320
Leonard and Sheldon have boxes of comics across the hall.

320
00:15:50,320 --> 00:15:51,520
Why don't we go look at those?

321
00:15:51,520 --> 00:15:53,240
Oh, great! Yes! And then you will see.

322
00:15:53,240 --> 00:15:56,000
I am not wrong, because if we were in outer space,

323
00:15:56,000 --> 00:15:57,490
then anyone could pick up the hammer

324
00:15:57,490 --> 00:16:00,460
because it would be floating around in a weightless environment.

325
00:16:00,460 --> 00:16:02,580
Yeah, that's right, the slow reader used science.

326
00:16:02,580 --> 00:16:04,020
Suck on that.

327
00:16:07,290 --> 00:16:08,870
Was there anything valuable in the car?

328
00:16:08,870 --> 00:16:11,480
Our clothes, our wallets, phones.

329
00:16:11,480 --> 00:16:13,450
And about $300 worth of makeup,

330
00:16:13,450 --> 00:16:16,630
so this thief could look like anyone right now.

331
00:16:18,110 --> 00:16:19,230
Makeup?

332
00:16:19,230 --> 00:16:21,040
Sure.

333
00:16:22,950 --> 00:16:25,580
Uh, we're going to the Comic-Con in Bakersfield.

334
00:16:25,580 --> 00:16:28,220
They have a big costume contest.

335
00:16:28,220 --> 00:16:30,570
It's cooler than it sounds.

336
00:16:31,070 --> 00:16:34,220
I don't know, sounds pretty cool.

337
00:16:35,330 --> 00:16:36,740
I-It is.

338
00:16:36,740 --> 00:16:37,850
It's not just comic books.

339
00:16:37,850 --> 00:16:39,580
They've got action figures, toys,

340
00:16:39,580 --> 00:16:41,120
a Tron-themed dance party...

341
00:16:41,120 --> 00:16:42,820
Okay, he gets how cool it is.

342
00:16:44,160 --> 00:16:45,890
I think I have all I need here.

343
00:16:45,890 --> 00:16:47,550
You guys need me to call someone?

344
00:16:47,550 --> 00:16:50,390
I'm guessing your moms?

345
00:16:51,760 --> 00:16:54,390
Thanks, but we've got it covered.

346
00:16:54,390 --> 00:16:56,800
Okay, I just talked to my mom.

347
00:17:01,100 --> 00:17:03,150
She arranged for us to get a rental car.

348
00:17:03,150 --> 00:17:04,000
Great.

349
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:05,640
We can still make it to Comic-Con.

350
00:17:05,640 --> 00:17:06,920
Are you kidding me?

351
00:17:06,920 --> 00:17:09,210
After all we've been through, I just want to go home.

352
00:17:09,210 --> 00:17:10,610
Don't be like that.

353
00:17:10,610 --> 00:17:11,980
Come on, Howard, talk to him.

354
00:17:11,980 --> 00:17:13,480
I'm with Leonard.

355
00:17:13,480 --> 00:17:15,510
I'm done.

356
00:17:15,510 --> 00:17:18,150
Fine, then I guess it's two against two.

357
00:17:18,150 --> 00:17:19,550
How do we decide?

358
00:17:21,590 --> 00:17:23,680
Actually, it's three against one.

359
00:17:23,680 --> 00:17:24,560
What?!

360
00:17:24,560 --> 00:17:25,960
What about the mission?

361
00:17:25,960 --> 00:17:27,790
You said we were a real-life landing party.

362
00:17:27,790 --> 00:17:29,190
Yeah, well, we're not.

363
00:17:29,660 --> 00:17:31,940
We're an imaginary landing party

364
00:17:31,940 --> 00:17:33,860
who had real-life garbage thrown at us

365
00:17:33,860 --> 00:17:36,400
by real-life strangers who think we're idiots.

366
00:17:37,800 --> 00:17:39,140
And to tell you the truth,

367
00:17:39,140 --> 00:17:41,440
I'm starting to feel like one.

368
00:17:41,440 --> 00:17:42,930
I want to go home now.

369
00:17:44,820 --> 00:17:46,520
Okay.

370
00:17:46,520 --> 00:17:49,610
Did we at least rent the car from Enterprise?

371
00:17:52,860 --> 00:17:55,110
Get it? Enterprise?

372
00:17:56,190 --> 00:17:58,650
Screw you. That's funny.

373
00:18:01,960 --> 00:18:03,680
Well, what if the Hulk picked up Thor

374
00:18:03,680 --> 00:18:05,730
while Thor is holding the hammer?

375
00:18:05,730 --> 00:18:06,520
Yeah?

376
00:18:06,520 --> 00:18:08,680
Then by the transitive property of picking things up,

377
00:18:08,680 --> 00:18:10,860
Hulk picked up the hammer.

378
00:18:12,240 --> 00:18:13,060
No.

379
00:18:13,060 --> 00:18:15,450
Hulk picked up Thor, Thor picked up the hammer.

380
00:18:15,450 --> 00:18:16,460
Okay, hang on.

381
00:18:16,460 --> 00:18:18,380
If I go to a bar and pick up a guy,

382
00:18:18,380 --> 00:18:19,940
and he picks up a girl, and then we all leave together,

383
00:18:19,940 --> 00:18:22,000
did I pick up the girl?

384
00:18:26,410 --> 00:18:28,340
Did that ever happen?

385
00:18:30,060 --> 00:18:32,530
Hey, are we talking about me or are we talking about Thor?

386
00:18:39,930 --> 00:18:42,900
Well, I say that's the last time we ever go outside.

387
00:18:43,740 --> 00:18:45,030
Look, right here.

388
00:18:45,030 --> 00:18:46,860
Red Hulk is picking up Thor's hammer

389
00:18:46,860 --> 00:18:48,750
'cause Thor's touching it.

390
00:18:48,750 --> 00:18:51,020
No, it's because they're in space.

391
00:18:51,020 --> 00:18:52,710
He's really just touching the strap.

392
00:18:52,710 --> 00:18:53,980
The strap is part of the hammer!

393
00:18:53,980 --> 00:18:56,020
No, it's not!

394
00:18:56,020 --> 00:18:59,370
Are they actually arguing about comic books?

395
00:18:59,370 --> 00:19:01,190
No, that can't be right.

396
00:19:01,190 --> 00:19:02,880
Maybe "Thor's Hammer"

397
00:19:02,880 --> 00:19:05,520
is a new color of nail polish.

398
00:19:06,860 --> 00:19:08,460
Then Red Hulk must be worthy.

399
00:19:08,460 --> 00:19:10,530
How could Red Hulk be worthy?

400
00:19:10,530 --> 00:19:12,600
You don't know his life!

401
00:19:17,470 --> 00:19:19,190
There's only one logical explanation.

402
00:19:19,190 --> 00:19:20,790
Somewhere in the desert

403
00:19:20,790 --> 00:19:23,700
we crossed into an alternate dimension...

404
00:19:23,710 --> 00:19:25,140
where the women in our lives

405
00:19:25,140 --> 00:19:27,840
can finally appreciate great literature.

406
00:19:29,310 --> 00:19:31,650
If it's an alternate dimension...

407
00:19:31,650 --> 00:19:34,820
sounds like a job for a landing party.

408
00:19:39,360 --> 00:19:41,690
Captain, what are your orders?

409
00:19:42,950 --> 00:19:44,500
I say we investigate.

410
00:19:48,270 --> 00:19:49,470
Wait.

411
00:19:51,640 --> 00:19:53,640
They might be hostile.

412
00:19:56,170 --> 00:19:57,770
Fine, but set them to stun.

413
00:19:57,780 --> 00:19:59,180
If we vaporize Penny,

414
00:19:59,180 --> 00:20:01,100
I'll never find a girlfriend that pretty again.

415
00:20:11,210 --> 00:20:12,880
Fun fact:

416
00:20:12,880 --> 00:20:16,770
President Eisenhower signed the Federal Aid Highway Act

417
00:20:16,770 --> 00:20:19,230
from his hospital room.

418
00:20:19,230 --> 00:20:22,360
Wow, that is interesting.

419
00:20:23,610 --> 00:20:25,980
You learn something new every day.

420
00:20:27,060 --> 00:20:30,180
Say, can you name the four state capitols

421
00:20:30,180 --> 00:20:33,210
that are not served by the interstate system?

422
00:20:33,210 --> 00:20:36,420
Ooh, another quiz. Yes!

